// ADVERSARIAL PROBE (infer-wrong lens, round 2, 2026-08-06): `compute_hot_params`
// Seed A (checker.rs ~1445-1465) marks a function's own fn-TYPED PARAMETER
// "hot" only when `walk_calls_block` finds a call whose CALLEE IDENTIFIER
// NAME is textually equal to the parameter's own name (`!is_method && *cname
// == p.name`). This is the mechanism the already-known
// `attack_container_param_alias_defeats_hotparam.kry` escape defeats for a
// CONTAINER-typed parameter (`Box.f`, Seed B). This probe targets the SAME
// literal-name-match blind spot but on Seed A itself -- a BARE fn-typed
// parameter (no container involved at all) aliased into a fresh local
// before being invoked.
//
// `apply_via_alias`'s body is `let g = f; return g()`. The only call
// `walk_calls_block` records is `(cname="g", ...)`; "g" never equals the
// parameter name "f", so Seed A never marks position 0 hot for
// `apply_via_alias`. Consequences, independent of each other:
//   (1) call-site defense: `main`'s call `apply_via_alias(reader)` consults
//       `hot_params["apply_via_alias"]` to decide whether to charge the
//       actual argument's traced authority via `accumulate_hot_extra_caps`
//       -- with no hot entry at position 0, nothing extra is charged.
//   (2) body-internal defense: `apply_via_alias`'s OWN inferred capability
//       set is computed by walking `g()`, which is NOT a known name, so it
//       falls into `resolve_direct_invoke_caps` -> `local_caps.get("g")`.
//       If `build_local_closure_caps` correctly threads `let g = f` to
//       `DependsOnParam("f")`, the function's own inferred set stays
//       (correctly) EMPTY, since a hot-parameter deferral to the CALLER is
//       the intended design (invariant 2) -- but that deferral is only
//       real when (1)'s call-site charge actually fires. Because Seed A
//       missed the alias, the deferral resolves to nothing: the requirement
//       silently disappears in both directions instead of surfacing at
//       either the callee or the caller.
//
// Positive control: `attack_bare_fn_param_alias_defeats_hotparam_control.kry`
// calls the parameter directly (`f()`, no alias) and IS correctly rejected
// -- confirming the alias is what defeats the guard, not something generic
// about a bare fn-typed parameter.

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

fn apply_via_alias(f: fn() -> str) -> str {
    let g = f
    return g()
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    deny!(fs:read) {
        let leaked = apply_via_alias(reader)
        println("BARE-FN-PARAM ALIAS LEAK: " + leaked)
        sleep(100)
    }
}
